7] [17592186362072 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186361336 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362072 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186361454 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362072 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186361544 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362072 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186361656 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362072 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362072 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362072 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186361544 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362072 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "69b59c33-2059-5031-b6c9-48e74e90a78e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362073 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186361546 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362073 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186361567 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362073 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186361694 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362073 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186361700 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362073 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186361751 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362073 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362073 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362073 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186361694 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362073 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "6a1f27a1-0eda-517d-a4eb-016e3810f8d3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362085 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186360976 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362085 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186361305 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362085 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186361480 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362085 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186361772 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362085 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186361822 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362085 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362085 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362085 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186361772 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362085 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "7ce9a233-37ed-5628-bbcf-882c2c09d5d9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362188 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186360926 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362188 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186361102 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362188 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186361421 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362188 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186361422 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362188 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186361456 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362188 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362188 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362188 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186361421 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362188 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "e828baa6-f3ec-5068-b0c9-1c1c3f9d8a66" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362342 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186362188 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362342 #edb/ref 1069 "q-99163" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362342 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362342 #edb/ref 1071 "A rectangular parallelepiped has a volume of $125\\,{\\text{in}}^{3}.$ Using the method of Lagrange multipliers, determine the minimum sum of the lengths of its edges." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362342 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1ec51af1-dbfa-5fa9-8f74-3c330eb163be" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362342 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ be the rectangular parallelepiped's length, width, and height, respectively. We wish to minimize the function\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=4x+4y+4z,\n$$\n\nsubject to the constraint \n\n$$\nxyz=125.\n$$\n\nNote that we also have the constraints $x>0,y>0,z>0.$\n\nLet $g(x,y,z)=xyz.$ Therefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y,z)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z),\\,g(x,y,z)=125\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y,z),{f}_{y}(x,y,z),{f}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y,z),{g}_{y}(x,y,z),{g}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ \\\\ ⟨4,4,4⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨yz,xz,xy⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}4=\\lambda yz \\\\ 4=\\lambda xz \\\\ 4=\\lambda xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying the first, second, and third equations by $x,y,$ and $z$ respectively, we get the following:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}4x=\\lambda xyz \\\\ 4y=\\lambda xyz \\\\ 4z=\\lambda xyz\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives $x=y=z.$\n\nNow, using the constraint equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}xyz & =125 \\\\ x(x)(x) & =125 \\\\ {x}^{3} & =125 \\\\ x & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of $f$ is reached at the point $(5,5,5),$ and the minimum value is \n\n$$\nf(5,5,5)=4(5)+4(5)+4(5)=60\\,\\text{in}.\n$$\n\n**Note:** It's easy to show that the obtained point is indeed the minimum (and not the maximum). To do that, we could compare the value of the function at this point with the value of the function at any other (arbitrary) point belonging to the domain. For example, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(25,5,1) & =4(25)+4(5)+4(1) \\\\  & =121 \\\\  & >f(5,5,5).\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362346 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186362085 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362346 #edb/ref 1069 "q-99240" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362346 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362346 #edb/ref 1071 "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the minimum value of the function $f(x,y)=5-x+2y$ given that $2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 4.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362346 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "85f60f32-4588-5316-bc63-974d85067877" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362346 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we need to find the critical values of the function in the region's interior. We compute the critical points by solving $∇f=0,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩ \\\\ ⟨-1,2⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the above equation has no solutions, we conclude that the function does not have critical values in the region's interior.\n\nNext, we need to find the minimum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)=2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y)\\,g(x,y)=4\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨-1,2⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨4x,2y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we cannot have $\\lambda =0$ since this leads to a contradiction in the above equation. Therefore, assuming $\\lambda \\ne 0,$ we have the following\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}-1=4\\lambda x \\\\ 2=2\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}x=-\\frac{1}{4\\lambda } \\\\ y=\\frac{1}{\\lambda }\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,x=-\\frac{1}{4}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\n  Substituting $x=-\\frac{1}{4}y$ into the condition $g(x,y)=4,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =4 \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{8}+{y}^{2} & =4 \\\\ {y}^{2} & =\\frac{32}{9} \\\\ y & =\\pm \\frac{4\\sqrt{2}}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{1}{4}y=∓\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},\\frac{4\\sqrt{2}}{3}),\\,(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},-\\frac{4\\sqrt{2}}{3}).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},\\frac{4\\sqrt{2}}{3})$ | $(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},-\\frac{4\\sqrt{2}}{3})$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $5+3\\sqrt{2}$ | $5-3\\sqrt{2}$ |\n\n \n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum value at $(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},-\\frac{4\\sqrt{2}}{3})\\,,$ and its minimum value is $5-3\\sqrt{2}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362348 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186362073 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362348 #edb/ref 1069 "q-99255" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362348 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362348 #edb/ref 1071 "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the maximum value of the function $f(x,y)=4{x}^{2}+10{y}^{2}$ on the region ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 4.$ \n\n*Hint*: The critical point of $f(x,y)$ in the region ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}<4$ is $(0,0).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362348 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "37949110-2c01-56b7-86c7-c788694289e7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362348 #edb/ref 1074 "We are given that the critical point of $f(x,y)$ in the region's interior is $(0,0).$\n\nNow, we need to find the maximum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=4\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨8x,20y⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,2y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}8x & =2\\lambda x \\\\ 20y & =2\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}x(4-\\lambda )=0 \\\\ y(10-\\lambda )=0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this system, we break it up into several cases:\n\n- Assuming $x=0$ in the first equation of the system, the constraint condition $g(x,y)=4$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =4\\,⇒\\,y=\\pm 2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(0,\\pm 2).$\n\n- Assuming $y=0$ in the second equation of the system, the constraint condition $g(x,y)=4$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2} & =4\\,⇒\\,x=\\pm 2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(\\pm 2,0).$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(0,\\pm 2),\\,(\\pm 2,0),\\,(0,0).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(0,\\pm 2)$ | $(\\pm 2,0)$ | $(0,0)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,f(x,y)\\,$ | $40$ | $16$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a maximum value at $(0,2)$ and $(0,-2),$ and its maximum value is $40.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364648 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186364649 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364648 #edb/ref 1069 "q-99230" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364648 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364648 #edb/ref 1071 "A rectangular metal tank without a lid should hold $256\\,{\\text{ft}}^{3}$ of liquid. Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the tank's minimum possible surface area." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364648 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9021d181-6a6e-589c-8096-c7ad92921266" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364648 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ be the tank's length, width, and height, respectively. We wish to minimize the function\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=xy+2xz+2yz,\n$$\n\nsubject to the constraint \n\n$$\nxyz=256.\n$$\n\nNote that we also have the constraints $x>0,y>0,z>0.$\n\nLet $g(x,y,z)=xyz.$ Therefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y,z)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z),\\,g(x,y,z)=256\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y,z),{f}_{y}(x,y,z),{f}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y,z),{g}_{y}(x,y,z),{g}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ \\\\ ⟨y+2z,x+2z,2x+2y⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨yz,xz,xy⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y+2z=\\lambda yz \\\\ x+2z=\\lambda xz \\\\ 2x+2y=\\lambda xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying the first, second, and third equations by $x,y,$ and $z$ respectively, we get the following:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x(y+2z)=\\lambda xyz \\\\ y(x+2z)=\\lambda xyz \\\\ z(2x+2y)=\\lambda xyz\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first and second equations above, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(y+2z) & =y(x+2z) \\\\ xy+2xz & =xy+2yz \\\\ 2xz & =2yz \\\\ 2z(x-y) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $z>0,$ we can conclude that $x=y.$\n\nSimilarly, from the second and third equations, we get that $x=y=2z.$ So, using the constraint equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}xyz & =256 \\\\ (2z)(2z)z & =256 \\\\ 4{z}^{3} & =256 \\\\ {z}^{3} & =64 \\\\ z & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of $f$ is reached at the point $(8,8,4),$ and the minimum value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(8,8,4)=8(8)+2(8)(4)+2(8)(4)=192\\,{\\text{ft}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** It's easy to show that the obtained point is indeed the minimum (and not the maximum). To do that, we could compare the value of the function at this point with the value of the function at any other (arbitrary) point belonging to the domain. For example, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(16,16,1) & =(16)(16)+2(16)(1)+2(16)(1) \\\\  & =320 \\\\  & >f(8,8,4).\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364649 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364650 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364649 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364651 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364649 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364652 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364649 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364653 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364649 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364654 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364649 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364649 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364649 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186364654 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364649 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "6ae58a56-6faf-52c9-9561-c87d35603ea0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364650 #edb/ref 10